1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:04,000
The sunlight on your skin right now left the Sun eight minutes ago.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:11,997
But the energy inside it may have spent tens of thousands of years, maybe more than a hundred thousand, just fighting its way out of the Sun.

3
00:00:12,697 --> 00:00:15,250
Light is the fastest thing in the universe.

4
00:00:15,250 --> 00:00:24,512
It covers almost three hundred thousand kilometers every second, enough to circle the Earth seven and a half times before you finish saying the word second.

5
00:00:24,512 --> 00:00:26,649
Nothing with mass can ever catch it.

6
00:00:27,349 --> 00:00:36,763
The fastest machine we've ever built, NASA's Parker Solar Probe, reached about six hundred ninety thousand kilometers per hour as it skimmed past the Sun.

7
00:00:36,763 --> 00:00:40,064
Light is still more than fifteen hundred times faster.

8
00:00:40,764 --> 00:00:42,288
And yet, light is slow.

9
00:00:42,288 --> 00:00:43,282
Painfully slow.

10
00:00:43,282 --> 00:00:45,535
I'll prove it with seven messages.

11
00:00:45,535 --> 00:00:50,637
We'll start inside your phone, and end at a place no message will ever reach.

12
00:00:50,637 --> 00:00:52,625
Seven messages, one stopwatch.

13
00:00:53,325 --> 00:00:54,775
The Speed of Light Is Slow.

14
00:00:55,475 --> 00:00:56,187
Message one.

15
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,675
Inside the device you're watching this on.

16
00:00:59,375 --> 00:01:03,171
A modern processor ticks about three billion times every second.

17
00:01:03,171 --> 00:01:06,610
In one of those ticks, light travels just ten centimeters.

18
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:08,745
That's about the width of your hand.

19
00:01:08,745 --> 00:01:14,142
Even the light from your screen needs more than a billionth of a second to reach your eyes.

20
00:01:14,142 --> 00:01:18,768
And the electrical signals inside a chip are slower than light in empty space.

21
00:01:19,468 --> 00:01:21,952
So chip designers fight over millimeters.

22
00:01:21,952 --> 00:01:26,194
A signal simply can't run across a big chip and back in a single tick.

23
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:33,830
That's one reason processors are tiny and packed tight, and why memory sits as close to the processor as engineers can put it.

24
00:01:33,830 --> 00:01:38,859
At the scale of a computer, the speed of light is already a wall you can bump into.

25
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:40,307
Message two.

26
00:01:40,307 --> 00:01:42,674
A stock trade from New York to London.

27
00:01:43,374 --> 00:01:47,628
The two cities are about fifty five hundred seventy kilometers apart.

28
00:01:47,628 --> 00:01:51,758
In a vacuum, light would cross that in about nineteen milliseconds.

29
00:01:51,758 --> 00:01:59,033
But the internet runs through glass fiber, and inside glass, light slows down to roughly two thirds of its full speed.

30
00:01:59,733 --> 00:02:05,885
So by our calculation, even a perfectly straight cable needs about twenty seven milliseconds, one way.

31
00:02:05,885 --> 00:02:08,238
Real cables take detours, so it's more.

32
00:02:08,238 --> 00:02:19,879
That sounds like nothing, until you learn that trading firms have paid for straighter cables and chains of microwave towers, because radio through air travels almost at the full speed of light.

33
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:23,559
Fortunes are won and lost over a few thousandths of a second.

34
00:02:23,559 --> 00:02:31,641
And every time you play an online game against someone on another continent, part of the lag you feel is simply light taking its time.

35
00:02:32,341 --> 00:02:33,199
Message three.

36
00:02:33,199 --> 00:02:34,731
A phone call to the Moon.

37
00:02:35,431 --> 00:02:39,606
The Moon is about three hundred eighty four thousand kilometers away.

38
00:02:39,606 --> 00:02:48,018
Light needs about one point three seconds to get there, so every answer arrives at least two and a half seconds after you ask the question.

39
00:02:48,018 --> 00:02:53,766
You can hear those pauses in the Apollo recordings, between mission control and the astronauts.

40
00:02:53,766 --> 00:02:56,913
It's awkward, but you can still have a conversation.

41
00:02:56,913 --> 00:02:59,152
Scientists use this delay on purpose.

42
00:02:59,152 --> 00:03:05,567
Apollo astronauts left mirrors on the Moon, and observatories still fire lasers at them and time the echo.

43
00:03:05,567 --> 00:03:10,710
Today, that round trip measures the distance to the Moon to within a few millimeters.

44
00:03:11,410 --> 00:03:12,287
Message four.

45
00:03:12,287 --> 00:03:13,906
Driving a rover on Mars.

46
00:03:14,606 --> 00:03:20,193
Earth and Mars circle the Sun at different speeds, so the distance between them keeps changing.

47
00:03:20,193 --> 00:03:27,427
Depending on where the two planets are, a radio signal takes somewhere between about three and twenty two minutes, one way.

48
00:03:27,427 --> 00:03:31,250
Nobody can drive a rover with a joystick and a twenty minute lag.

49
00:03:31,250 --> 00:03:34,543
By the time you saw the rock, you'd already have hit it.

50
00:03:34,543 --> 00:03:40,600
That's why rovers get their instructions as a plan for the whole day, and then mostly drive themselves.

51
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:49,245
And when the Sun passes between Earth and Mars, it blocks the signal entirely for about two weeks, and the rovers are left completely on their own.

52
00:03:49,945 --> 00:03:58,066
In February twenty twenty one, NASA's Perseverance rover had about seven minutes to get from the top of the Martian atmosphere to the ground.

53
00:03:58,066 --> 00:04:01,060
Its signal took about eleven minutes to reach Earth.

54
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:10,962
So when the engineers heard that the rover was entering the atmosphere, it had already been on the ground for minutes, landed safely or smashed into the rock.

55
00:04:10,962 --> 00:04:12,535
Nobody on Earth could help.

56
00:04:12,535 --> 00:04:18,592
Every move had to be decided by the rover itself, because light was simply too slow to let humans steer.

57
00:04:19,292 --> 00:04:20,171
Message five.

58
00:04:20,171 --> 00:04:21,253
Not one we send.

59
00:04:21,253 --> 00:04:22,268
One we receive.

60
00:04:22,268 --> 00:04:22,876
Sunlight.

61
00:04:23,576 --> 00:04:30,398
The Sun is about a hundred fifty million kilometers away, so its light takes about eight minutes and nineteen seconds to reach you.

62
00:04:30,398 --> 00:04:34,669
Every time you look at the Sun's disc, you're looking eight minutes into the past.

63
00:04:34,669 --> 00:04:40,032
If the Sun vanished right now, you'd keep seeing it and feeling its warmth for more than eight minutes.

64
00:04:40,032 --> 00:04:46,125
Even its gravity, which also travels at the speed of light, would keep Earth on its old path for those eight minutes.

65
00:04:46,825 --> 00:04:49,010
But that's the easy part of the trip.

66
00:04:49,010 --> 00:04:53,794
The energy in sunlight is made in the Sun's core, and getting out is a nightmare.

67
00:04:53,794 --> 00:05:00,525
Inside, the Sun is so dense that light is absorbed and given off again and again, each time in a random direction.

68
00:05:00,525 --> 00:05:05,663
In a straight line, the trip from the core to the surface would take about two seconds.

69
00:05:06,363 --> 00:05:12,713
Instead, according to NASA, that energy can take around a hundred seventy thousand years to work its way out.

70
00:05:12,713 --> 00:05:18,014
The exact number depends on the model, and some estimates are closer to ten thousand years.

71
00:05:18,014 --> 00:05:23,023
Either way, the warmth on your face began its journey long before anyone built a city.

72
00:05:23,023 --> 00:05:24,655
Eight minutes through space.

73
00:05:24,655 --> 00:05:26,693
Thousands of years through the Sun.

74
00:05:27,393 --> 00:05:28,161
Message six.

75
00:05:28,161 --> 00:05:30,913
A call to the most distant spacecraft ever.

76
00:05:31,613 --> 00:05:36,124
Voyager one left Earth in nineteen seventy seven and it's still talking to us.

77
00:05:36,124 --> 00:05:40,518
Right now it's so far away that a command takes almost a full day to arrive.

78
00:05:40,518 --> 00:05:45,954
In a few days, on November eighteenth, it crosses one light-day from Earth for the first time.

79
00:05:45,954 --> 00:05:49,886
Ask it a question on Monday, and the answer comes back on Wednesday.

80
00:05:49,886 --> 00:05:55,379
When engineers fix a problem on Voyager, they wait two days to find out whether the fix worked.

81
00:05:56,079 --> 00:05:58,512
And Voyager is basically still next door.

82
00:05:58,512 --> 00:06:03,021
The nearest star, Proxima Centauri, is four point two four light-years away.

83
00:06:03,021 --> 00:06:08,955
Say hello today, and the earliest possible reply would arrive about eight and a half years from now.

84
00:06:08,955 --> 00:06:13,762
The light from Proxima reaching Earth tonight left the star in twenty twenty two.

85
00:06:13,762 --> 00:06:19,161
A round trip across our galaxy, the Milky Way, would take about two hundred thousand years.

86
00:06:19,861 --> 00:06:22,262
That slowness has a strange side effect.

87
00:06:22,262 --> 00:06:25,323
Every time you look up, you're looking at the past.

88
00:06:25,323 --> 00:06:33,005
The Andromeda galaxy, on a dark night, is light that left it about two and a half million years ago, before our species existed.

89
00:06:33,005 --> 00:06:34,685
You're not seeing Andromeda.

90
00:06:34,685 --> 00:06:37,205
You're seeing a very old photograph of it.

91
00:06:37,905 --> 00:06:38,720
Message seven.

92
00:06:38,720 --> 00:06:40,465
The one that can never arrive.

93
00:06:41,165 --> 00:06:42,783
The universe is expanding.

94
00:06:42,783 --> 00:06:48,132
The space between distant galaxies keeps stretching, and the expansion is speeding up.

95
00:06:48,132 --> 00:06:54,413
Picture light from a far away galaxy as a runner on a treadmill that keeps getting longer and faster.

96
00:06:55,113 --> 00:07:01,993
For galaxies closer than about sixteen billion light-years, the light they send today will reach us eventually.

97
00:07:01,993 --> 00:07:06,021
Beyond that line, called the cosmic event horizon, it never will.

98
00:07:06,021 --> 00:07:07,447
Not in a billion years.

99
00:07:07,447 --> 00:07:08,004
Not ever.

100
00:07:08,004 --> 00:07:12,095
And as time goes on, more and more galaxies slip across that line.

101
00:07:12,795 --> 00:07:20,261
We can still see plenty of galaxies beyond that line, because we're catching old light they sent long ago, when they were closer.

102
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:27,495
But by our rough calculation, more than nine out of ten galaxies in the observable universe are already out of reach forever.

103
00:07:27,495 --> 00:07:31,547
Even a message sent today at the speed of light would never get there.

104
00:07:32,247 --> 00:07:34,968
So yes, light is the fastest thing there is.

105
00:07:34,968 --> 00:07:43,380
It's also too slow for your processor, too slow for traders, too slow to drive on Mars, and too slow to ever reach most of the universe.

106
00:07:43,380 --> 00:07:45,792
The cosmic speed limit isn't just fast.

107
00:07:45,792 --> 00:07:46,535
It's a wall.

108
00:07:46,535 --> 00:07:50,679
Every number in this video, with its source, is in the description.

109
00:07:51,379 --> 00:07:53,514
And if light is slow, imagine us.

110
00:07:53,514 --> 00:08:02,571
Next time: how long would it take to travel to every planet in the solar system, by car, by plane, and by the fastest spacecraft ever built?

111
00:08:02,571 --> 00:08:03,347
That's next.
